      "writtenOn": "2020-01-28T14:36:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Creating a new thread seems fine, but is there a reason we can\u0027t use the singleton ConnectivityThread? I think it would be something like:\n\n    final Handler handler \u003d ConnectivityThread.get().getThreadHandler();\n    final Runnable r \u003d () -\u003e {\n        final IBinder connector \u003d mConnectorSupplier.get();\n            if (connector !\u003d null) {\n                onTetheringConnected(ITetheringConnector.Stub.asInterface(connector));\n                return;\n            }\n            handler.postDelayed(r, CONNECTOR_POLL_INTERVAL_MILLIS);\n        }\n    }\n\nUsing the ConnectivityThread means that if that thread is busy, then starting tetheringmanager could be slow. Also, it means that if the ConnectivityThread is blocked on a lock (e.g., because the app is taking a lock from NetworkCallback that was registered with a null Handler, and expects to release the lock when the TetheringManager is created, it could deadlock. This scenario seems pretty far-fetched but there could be others.",
